.Dd November 19, 2026
.Dt TMPFS 5
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm tmpfs
.Nd a file system in memory
.Sh DESCRIPTION
A tmpfs keeps its files in memory: it has no device, nothing of it is
written to a disk, and its files go when it is unmounted or the machine
stops.
It has every kind of node: regular files, directories, symbolic links,
hard links, FIFOs, socket files and device nodes, as Linux's tmpfs has.
.Pp
The kernel mounts one at
.Pa /dev/shm
at boot, mode 1777 with
.Ql nosuid
and
.Ql nodev ,
where the C libraries'
.Fn shm_open
and
.Fn sem_open
make their files, and keeps another, which no path reaches, for the files
of
.Xr memfd_create 2
and the segments of System V shared memory.
The metal and recovery builds of
.Xr init 8
mount one at
.Pa /tmp
with the options of Debian 13's
.Pa tmp.mount :
mode 1777, strictatime, nosuid and nodev, at most half the machine's
memory and 1048576 inodes.
What a program must keep across a reboot belongs in
.Pa /var/tmp ,
on the root.
.Pp
.Xr mount 2
places another, of type
.Ql tmpfs ,
with any source, conventionally
.Ql tmpfs
or
.Ql none .
It takes these options, comma-separated:
.Bl -tag -width "nr_inodes=N"
.It Ql mode= Ns Ar M
The root directory's mode, in octal; 1777 without the option.
.It Ql size= Ns Ar N
The most the files may hold: bytes, with
.Ql k ,
.Ql m
or
.Ql g
after them, or a percentage of the machine's memory with
.Ql % ,
rounded up to whole pages; 0 is no limit, and without the option the
limit is half the machine's memory.
A write past it fails with
.Er ENOSPC .
.It Ql nr_inodes= Ns Ar N
The most inodes the mount may have, the root's among them: a count, with
.Ql k ,
.Ql m ,
.Ql g ,
.Ql t ,
.Ql p
or
.Ql e
after it for a power of 1024; 0 is no limit, and without the option the
limit is half the machine's pages.
As on Linux, the limit is kept as space of 1024 bytes for each inode:
each node takes one, each name of a file past its first another, and an
extended attribute 40 bytes and the bytes of its name and value.
What does not fit fails with
.Er ENOSPC .
.El
.Pp
A value of
.Ql size=
or
.Ql nr_inodes=
that is no number fails the mount with
.Er EINVAL ;
the other options Linux's tmpfs takes are accepted and change nothing.
A remount takes a new
.Ql size=
and
.Ql nr_inodes= ,
and changes neither and fails with
.Er EINVAL
when either is smaller than what the files hold or sets a limit on a
mount that has none; 0 lifts its limit for good.
.Xr statfs 2
reports
.Dv TMPFS_MAGIC ,
0x01021994, blocks of a page, the size limit as the blocks and the pages
the files hold as those in use, and the inode limit and the whole inodes
left of its space as
.Va f_files
and
.Va f_ffree ,
or 0 for both on a mount with no inode limit.
.Pp
A regular file's pages are made when it is first written or mapped, so a
hole costs nothing, and a file reaches 16 TiB less a page.
.Xr fallocate 2
makes pages and punches holes, and
.Xr fcntl 2
seals a file, as on Linux.
.Pp
A file or a directory keeps the inode flags of
.Xr ioctl 2 Ns 's
.Dv FS_IOC_SETFLAGS
that Linux's tmpfs keeps: immutable, 0x10, append-only, 0x20, no-dump,
0x40, and no-access-time, 0x80; any other fails with
.Er EOPNOTSUPP .
A new node takes the last two from its directory, and a read of a file
with no-access-time leaves its access time.
.Dv FS_IOC_GETVERSION
fails with
.Er ENOTTY ,
since a tmpfs keeps no generation.
.Pp
Every node keeps extended attributes of the
.Li user. ,
.Li trusted.
and
.Li security.
namespaces, as
.Xr getxattr 2
gives them, in the order they were made; the
.Li system.
namespace, Linux's POSIX ACLs, fails with
.Er EOPNOTSUPP .
.Sh EXAMPLES
As root:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# mkdir /mnt/scratch
# mount -t tmpfs -o size=64m,nr_inodes=4k tmpfs /mnt/scratch
# stat -f -c '%c inodes, %d free' /mnt/scratch
4096 inodes, 4095 free
# umount /mnt/scratch
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr fallocate 2 ,
.Xr getxattr 2 ,
.Xr ioctl 2 ,
.Xr memfd_create 2 ,
.Xr mount 2 ,
.Xr statfs 2 ,
.Xr debugfs 5 ,
.Xr proc 5 ,
.Xr sysfs 5 ,
.Xr init 8 ,
.Xr mount 8
